    long: "Article 17 of the GDPR gives the data subject the right to obtain erasure from the controller where, among other grounds, the data are no longer necessary for the purpose they were collected for, consent is withdrawn and no other legal ground remains, or the data were unlawfully processed. The right has limits. It does not apply where processing is necessary to comply with a legal obligation, for legal claims, or for certain public interest and research purposes. Where the controller has made the data public, it must take reasonable steps to inform other controllers that are processing it. The duty assumes a controller that can delete. On an append-only ledger or permanent storage nothing can be removed, so the decision is made before the first write: which data may go on the permanent layer at all.",
