    short: "Turning stored or transmitted bytes back into live objects. When the bytes choose the types, a data channel becomes a code channel.",
    long: "Every program that reads a saved session, a message from a queue or a cached object deserializes it. The danger is in formats where the input names the type to build: constructing an attacker-chosen type can run code during reconstruction, using classes already present in the application's libraries. Unsafe deserialization turns a data channel into a code channel. Every mainstream deserialization library now supports a type allowlist, and the curriculum notes that every one ships with it disabled for backwards compatibility. Memory-safe languages have their own deserialization flaws. The curriculum's drill uses a deserialization finding because it shows the widest gap between a scanner signature and actual exploitability: a critical deserialization CVE in a code path nothing calls carries roughly the risk of a comment. The vulnerability class is described under Insecure Deserialization.",
