    short: "A statement that a technique or behaviour is detected. The curriculum accepts one only with evidence that the detection fires on known-true data.",
    long: "Coverage claims are easy to make and hard to earn. The pyramid of pain Step says they should be made per analytic with a level attached, and that a technique counts as covered only when at least one validated analytic at Level 4 or Level 5 exists for it. A portfolio of two hundred Level 1 detections and four Level 5 detections is not ninety-eight percent covered; it has four detections and a large archive. The detection funnel Step adds that coverage claims are made about the funnel's fourth stage while breaches happen at its second, third and sixth. In the AI detection lab, coverage claims must be earned by replay, not by rule count: a detection that has never fired against known-true data is a coverage claim with no evidence. A hunt that finds nothing in sufficient data records a claim of its own, scoped to that behaviour, that data and that period. In the ADS document format, the Blind Spots and Assumptions section is the one that keeps coverage claims honest.",
