    term: "Penetration Test",
    domain: "Assurance",
    short: "A time-boxed attempt by skilled testers to break into a system with permission, ending in a report of what they found.",
    long: "The curriculum's drill sets the scene two days before a launch, with the report already in a shared drive the programme director can read, and gives the rule: a penetration test report is an input to a decision, not the decision. A typical report from a competent firm holds a handful of findings that would change the risk materially, a larger set that are real but of low consequence, and a tail of informational items driven by the firm's checklist. Reading one means asking three things of each finding. Does it reproduce in the environment that will ship? Does it need a precondition the organisation already controls, such as an authenticated session or a position inside a segment? And is it an instance of a class that can be retired? The game day Step draws the contrast with its own exercise: a red team asks whether an adversary can get in, while a game day asks whether your defences behave the way you have documented. Application tests also have blind spots; they do not detect build compromises.",
