    id: "recall",
    term: "Recall",
    domain: "Operations Metrics",
    short: "The share of the real problems that a tool or detection finds: the number that hurts, and the one that cannot be measured without ground truth.",
    long: "Precision asks how many of a tool's findings are real; recall asks how many of the real problems it found. The curriculum's false-positive Step puts them side by side. Precision is the number you can measure, by hand-triaging a sample, which is exactly why programmes over-optimise for it. Recall is the number that will hurt you, and you cannot measure it without a ground truth you do not have. The only practical proxies are findings from penetration tests and bug bounties that the tools had the opportunity to catch and did not, and post-incident analysis of whether the exploited defect sat in a scanned path. The advice is to track both, present both, and be suspicious of any tool report that only ever discusses what was found.",
